Orbital AI Infrastructure:

A First‑Principles Framework for Physics, Engineering, and Strategic Viability

Final White Paper — June 2026

---

Abstract

The migration of artificial intelligence compute infrastructure to Earth orbit has evolved from speculative fiction to operational reality in under three years. Multiple operators now possess in‑orbit hardware, and plans for megaconstellations of up to one million data‑centre satellites are under regulatory review. This paper posits that the defining challenge of orbital AI is not computation itself, but the simultaneous closure of an Energy–Thermal–Communication triangle, now extended by rigorous treatment of orbital mechanics, scaling laws, radiation effects, autonomy requirements, systemic risk, and the potential of in‑situ manufacturing. We derive first‑principles mathematical models for every major constraint, introduce the Compute‑to‑Communication Ratio (CCR) and Heat‑to‑Compute Ratio (HCR) as fundamental viability metrics, and propose the Orbital Viability Index (OVI) — a single composite index for cross‑architecture comparison. An enhanced Orbital AI Readiness Framework (OARF) is formulated as a quantitative tool. Finally, we articulate a Principle of Compute Migration that frames orbital AI as an inevitable stage in the physical evolution of computation. The paper does not advocate for or against the sector; it provides the physics‑grounded map that engineers, investors, and policymakers require to navigate the design space.

---

1. Introduction

The global AI industry is approaching a resource wall. The International Energy Agency projects that data‑centre electricity consumption could exceed 1 000 TWh by 2026, equivalent to the total demand of Japan. Water usage for cooling has become ecologically and politically contentious, and the permitting of new hyperscale facilities now routinely spans five to ten years in many jurisdictions. Concurrently, the cost of launching mass to low Earth orbit (LEO) has collapsed by two orders of magnitude — from over $40 000 kg⁻¹ in the shuttle era to approximately $2 500 kg⁻¹ on Falcon 9, with SpaceX’s Starship targeting $100–300 kg⁻¹. This new cost regime transforms space from a boutique domain into a potential extension of the global compute fabric.

Orbital data centres offer three inherent physical advantages: (i) continuous solar irradiance of 1 361 W m⁻² with no atmospheric attenuation, (ii) the ability to reject waste heat directly into the 4 K cosmic background, and (iii) unlimited physical expansion volume. These advantages have attracted significant capital: the in‑orbit data centre market is projected to reach $39 billion by 2035, and SpaceX alone has filed for up to one million satellites.

Yet the same physics that provides the advantages also imposes absolute constraints. Heat rejection in a vacuum proceeds only by radiation, governed by the Stefan–Boltzmann law. Communication to ground faces free‑space path loss and atmospheric attenuation. Launch mass — and thus cost — scales with every subsystem. This paper systematically analyses these constraints, establishes a formal mathematical architecture, and develops a suite of metrics that allow any proposed orbital AI system to be evaluated against the immutable laws of physics.

The paper is structured as a layered constraint analysis. After establishing strategic and orbital‑mechanical prerequisites (Sections 2–3), we present the complete mathematical foundation (Section 4), then examine each physical and operational constraint in turn (Sections 5–13). An integrated quantitative framework (Sections 14–15) is followed by a historical theory of compute migration (Section 16) and a research agenda (Section 17).

---

2. Layer 0 — Strategic Necessity

Before any engineering analysis, the question of why compute should leave Earth must be addressed. Three converging vectors argue that large‑scale orbital compute is not merely an option but a strategic necessity for the medium term.

2.1 Energy Thresholds
Global AI energy demand is on a super‑exponential trajectory. Training a single frontier model can consume gigawatt‑hours; inference at scale for billion‑user services will dominate future load. Terrestrial renewable expansion faces geographic, material, and grid‑stability constraints. Orbital solar power, delivered via a constellation in sun‑synchronous orbit, provides a constant, gigawatt‑scale source with a lifecycle carbon intensity of 7.5–14 gCO₂e kWh⁻¹, versus 200–500 gCO₂e kWh⁻¹ for most grids. If AI continues to grow at 30 % y⁻¹, terrestrial energy alone may become a binding constraint by the mid‑2030s; orbital generation is the only source that scales with the physical volume of the inner solar system.

2.2 Water and Land Scarcity
A 100 MW terrestrial data centre can consume over 4 million litres of water per day for evaporative cooling. In a warming climate, competition for freshwater between agriculture, human consumption, and industry will intensify. Orbital compute eliminates water use entirely. It also avoids the land‑use conflicts that increasingly block terrestrial data centre construction in Europe, North America, and parts of Asia.

2.3 Jurisdictional Neutrality
Computation performed in orbit occupies a legal grey zone not unambiguously subject to any single nation’s data sovereignty laws. For certain classes of workload — sovereign wealth computation, legally sensitive financial models, multi‑jurisdictional AI inference — this property can command a substantial price premium, potentially decoupling economic viability from raw hardware cost comparisons.

These factors do not guarantee economic viability; they establish that the effort to understand and overcome the physical constraints is strategically justified.

