3.3 Energy and Battery Mass
For LEO with eclipse fraction f_e, required battery storage per orbit is:

E_{\text{bat}} = P_{\text{load}} \cdot f_e \cdot T.

Battery mass:

M_{\text{bat}} = \frac{E_{\text{bat}}}{\rho_{\text{bat}}},

where \rho_{\text{bat}} is specific energy (150–250 Wh kg⁻¹ for Li‑ion, up to 400–500 for solid‑state). For a 1 MW load, 500 km LEO (f_e\approx0.35, T\approx94\ \text{min}), we obtain E_{\text{bat}}\approx 548\ \text{kWh} and M_{\text{bat}}\approx 2.2–3.7\ \text{tonnes}. This mass penalty is significant but manageable; for SSO it disappears, making SSO the preferred initial orbit for large‑scale compute.

3.4 Drag and Station‑Keeping
Atmospheric drag in VLEO requires continuous propulsion, adding propellant mass. Below ~400 km, station‑keeping costs become prohibitive for long‑duration missions without advanced air‑breathing electric propulsion. This imposes a lower practical altitude boundary for permanent data centres.

