4. Mathematical Foundations

We now establish the complete set of governing equations. All variables are defined in Appendix A; we use SI units throughout.

4.1 Thermal Radiation (Stefan–Boltzmann Law)

P_{\text{rad}} = \varepsilon \sigma A (T^4 - T_{\text{sink}}^4),
\tag{1}

with \varepsilon emissivity, \sigma = 5.670374419\times10^{-8}\ \text{W m}^{-2}\text{K}^{-4}. The effective sink temperature T_{\text{sink}} is not 4 K but a function of Earth infrared and solar albedo loading. For a radiator facing deep space in LEO, typical values are T_{\text{sink}} \in [180, 220]\ \text{K}; for a Sun‑facing surface, T_{\text{sink}} can exceed 400 K.

4.2 Solar Power Generation

P_{\text{solar}} = \eta \, G_{\text{sc}} \, A_{\text{panel}} \, (1-d)^t \, \cos\theta,
\tag{2}

where \eta\approx 0.28–0.34 (triple‑junction GaAs), G_{\text{sc}}=1361\ \text{W m}^{-2}, d\approx 0.02–0.03 yr⁻¹ degradation, t in years, \cos\theta the cosine of the Sun angle.

4.3 Link Budget — Optical Communication

P_{\text{rx}} = P_{\text{tx}} \, G_{\text{tx}} \, G_{\text{rx}} \, \left(\frac{\lambda}{4\pi d}\right)^2 \, \eta_{\text{atm}},
\tag{3}

where G = (\pi D/\lambda)^2 for aperture diameter D, \lambda wavelength (typically 1550 nm), d slant range, and \eta_{\text{atm}} the atmospheric transmission (clear sky ~0.7–0.9, clouded ~0).

Data rate per beam (Shannon limit approximation for optical with high SNR):

R \approx B \log_2(1+\text{SNR}), \quad \text{SNR} = \frac{P_{\text{rx}}}{N_0 B},

with B the optical bandwidth, N_0 photon shot noise.

4.4 Compute‑to‑Communication Ratio (CCR)

\text{CCR} = \frac{\text{Compute Operations (FLOP)}}{\text{Bits Transmitted to/from Ground}}.
\tag{4}

CCR determines workload suitability. Space‑native sensor processing yields CCR \gtrsim 10^6; training a large language model from Earth‑sourced data yields CCR \ll 1 because exabytes of data must be moved. A workload is viable if:

\text{CCR} \gg \frac{\text{Compute Power (FLOP/s)}}{\text{Available Downlink Bandwidth (bps)}}.
\tag{5}

4.5 Heat‑to‑Compute Ratio (HCR)

\text{HCR} = \frac{P_{\text{waste}}}{\text{FLOP/s}},
\tag{6}

where P_{\text{waste}} is the total waste heat produced by the compute subsystem. For a given accelerator, HCR is effectively the inverse of computational efficiency. HCR directly determines radiator mass:

M_{\text{rad}} \propto P_{\text{waste}} \propto \text{HCR} \times \text{FLOP/s}.

Thus, architectures with lower HCR are disproportionately more viable in orbit, as they reduce the dominant mass penalty. HCR becomes a key selection criterion for orbital‑grade AI silicon.

4.6 Mass Scaling Laws

For a system of total compute power P_{\text{comp}} (FLOP/s equivalent via power), the total system mass M_{\text{sys}} can be decomposed:

M_{\text{sys}} = M_{\text{comp}} + M_{\text{power}} + M_{\text{thermal}} + M_{\text{comm}} + M_{\text{struct}}.
\tag{7}

Empirically, each subsystem scales as:

\begin{aligned}
M_{\text{comp}} &\approx k_c P_{\text{comp}},\\
M_{\text{power}} &\approx k_p P_{\text{comp}} / \eta_{\text{PV}},\\
M_{\text{thermal}} &\approx k_t \frac{P_{\text{waste}}}{T^3} \approx k_t \frac{\text{HCR}\cdot P_{\text{comp}}}{T^3},\\
M_{\text{comm}} &\approx k_m P_{\text{comp}} / \text{CCR}.
\end{aligned}

The coefficients k_c, k_p, k_t, k_m are technology‑dependent. The scaling of M_{\text{thermal}} \propto 1/T^3 arises from Eq. (1): required area A \propto P_{\text{waste}}/T^4, and radiator mass M_{\text{rad}} \propto A (for fixed areal density). Thus raising operating temperature dramatically reduces thermal mass.

4.7 Orbital Viability Index (OVI)

We define a composite metric that condenses the key physical and economic variables:

\text{OVI} = \frac{(\text{Available Solar Power}) \times \text{CCR} \times \text{Reliability Factor}}{(\text{Thermal Cost}) \times (\text{Launch Cost per kg}) \times (\text{Systemic Risk Factor})}.
\tag{8}

Each term is normalised to a reference architecture. The OVI enables direct comparison between, e.g., a LEO sun‑synchronous droplet‑radiator design and a GEO solid‑radiator design. Detailed normalisation functions are provided in Appendix B.

