5. Layer 1 — The Energy Constraint

5.1 Solar Array Sizing
From Eq. (2), the required array area for a given electrical power P_{\text{elec}} at beginning‑of‑life is:

A_{\text{array}} = \frac{P_{\text{elec}}}{\eta \, G_{\text{sc}} \, \cos\theta}.

Assuming \eta=0.30, \cos\theta=0.8 (average over orbit), we obtain A_{\text{array}} \approx 5.5\ \text{m}^2 per kW. Including a degradation margin of 15 % over five years, A_{\text{array}} \approx 6.5\ \text{m}^2\text{ kW}^{-1}. For 1 MW, this is 6 500 m² — roughly the area of a football pitch.

5.2 Specific Mass of Power Systems
State‑of‑the‑art satellite power systems achieve ~30–40 kg kW⁻¹ (Starlink V2 Mini). For 1 MW, that is 30–40 tonnes of solar infrastructure, plus 2–4 tonnes of batteries for LEO eclipse. Launch cost at $2 000 kg⁻¹ yields $64–88 million for power alone.

5.3 Scaling and Future Projections
Advances in thin‑film photovoltaics and inflatable structures could reduce specific mass to 10–15 kg kW⁻¹ by the mid‑2030s. When combined with $100 kg⁻¹ launch, the launch cost for 1 MW of power drops to $1–1.5 million, making energy economically competitive with terrestrial industrial rates.

---

6. Layer 2 — The Thermal Constraint

6.1 Radiator Sizing
From Eq. (1), for a given waste heat P_{\text{waste}}:

A_{\text{rad}} = \frac{P_{\text{waste}}}{\varepsilon\sigma (T^4 - T_{\text{sink}}^4)}.

For P_{\text{waste}} = 1\ \text{MW}, T=353\ \text{K}, T_{\text{sink}}=200\ \text{K}, \varepsilon=0.9, we find A_{\text{rad}} \approx 1\,230\ \text{m}^2. Raising T to 393 K reduces A_{\text{rad}} to ~790 m², a 36 % reduction. This fourth‑power sensitivity makes high‑temperature compute electronics a top research priority.

6.2 Radiator Mass Penalty
If radiator areal density is \rho_A (kg m⁻²), then M_{\text{rad}} = \rho_A A_{\text{rad}}. Current ISS radiators have \rho_A \approx 7.5\ \text{kg m}^{-2}, giving M_{\text{rad}} \approx 9.2\ \text{tonnes} at T=353\ \text{K}. Advanced deployable radiators aim for 2–3 kg m⁻², reducing this to ~2.5–3.7 tonnes. Liquid‑droplet radiators, which eliminate the solid panel entirely, could achieve effective \rho_A < 0.5\ \text{kg m}^{-2}, making thermal mass a minority contributor.

6.3 Heat Rejection Architecture
Two‑phase pumped loops using ammonia or propylene transport heat from chip packages to remote radiator panels. The thermal resistance budget from junction to radiator surface must stay below ~20 K to avoid derating. Detailed thermal resistance network analysis (Appendix C) shows this is achievable with current micro‑channel cold‑plate technology, albeit with a mass penalty for the fluid and pumps.

---

7. Layer 2.5 — The Scaling Constraint

Many technologies that work at kilowatt scale fail at megawatt scale. We formalise three scaling regimes:

· Regime I (10–100 kW): Passive thermal control sufficient; solid radiators viable; launch mass manageable with current vehicles.
· Regime II (100 kW–10 MW): Active thermal control required; deployable radiators necessary; launch cadence becomes a scheduling constraint; on‑orbit assembly may be needed.
· Regime III (>10 MW): Megastructure; liquid‑droplet radiators likely mandatory; in‑situ manufacturing for structural elements; dedicated heavy‑lift fleet.

7.1 Thermal Scaling Breakdown
Radiator area scales linearly with power but radiator mass scaling depends on structural efficiency. For solid panels, mass ~ area, so M_{\text{rad}} \propto P_{\text{waste}}. For a 10 MW data centre, solid radiators would mass ~100 tonnes, consuming an entire Starship payload just for cooling. This is the key scaling barrier that motivates droplet radiators.

7.2 Launch Cadence Constraint
A 1 GW constellation built with Starship (100 t per launch) would require thousands of launches. With a maximum cadence of a few hundred per year per pad, multi‑pad global operations become necessary. This introduces supply‑chain, geopolitical, and regulatory scaling challenges that must be modeled alongside physics.

---

8. Layer 3 — The Radiation Constraint

8.1 Environmental Model
The trapped proton and electron fluxes are given by the AP‑8 and AE‑8 models; galactic cosmic rays (GCR) follow the Badhwar–O’Neill model. For LEO, the South Atlantic Anomaly (SAA) dominates the dose. The total ionising dose (TID) rate behind shielding of thickness x (g cm⁻²) is approximately:

\dot{D}(x) = \dot{D}_0 \, e^{-x/x_0},

with x_0 \sim 20\text{--}40\ \text{g cm}^{-2} for trapped protons.

8.2 Shielding and GPU Tolerance
NASA testing shows COTS GPUs tolerate TID up to 60 Gy without permanent failure. For a five‑year mission at 1 600 km, the unshielded dose can be ~100 Gy yr⁻¹; a 20 mm aluminium + water shield (~50 g cm⁻²) reduces this to ~10 Gy yr⁻¹, well within tolerance. However, single‑event upsets (SEU) remain a probabilistic threat. The SEU rate \lambda for a given device in LEO is typically 10^{-4}–10^{-2} upsets device⁻¹ day⁻¹. For a 10 000‑GPU constellation, this translates to thousands of upsets per day, necessitating robust error correction.

8.3 Application‑Layer Mitigation
Redundancy at the application layer can exploit the fault‑tolerant nature of neural networks. RedNet‑style architectures apply TMR only to sensitive layers, reducing compute overhead to 10–30 % while suppressing error propagation to near zero. This is a critical enabler for using COTS GPUs in orbit.

---

9. Layer 4 — The Communication Constraint

9.1 Link Budget and Data Rate
For a 600 km LEO optical downlink with 0.3 m telescope, 10 W transmit power, 1550 nm, the received power is ~10⁻⁸ W. With a photon‑counting receiver, achievable data rates are 10–100 Gbps per beam under clear sky. Cloud cover causes outages; a global network of 10–20 ground stations with adaptive optics can provide >99.9 % availability.

9.2 CCR‑Based Workload Partitioning
We propose a formal criterion: a workload W is orbit‑viable if:

\text{CCR}(W) > \frac{P_{\text{comp}}}{\text{DL}_{\text{agg}}},

where \text{DL}_{\text{agg}} is the aggregate downlink bandwidth of the constellation. For a 20 MW inference centre with \text{DL}_{\text{agg}} = 20\ \text{Tbps}, and P_{\text{comp}} = 10^{18}\ \text{FLOP/s} (approx), the threshold CCR is 5 \times 10^4. Earth‑observation inference (CCR ~10⁸) passes comfortably; LLM training (CCR ~10⁻²) fails by seven orders of magnitude.

9.3 Inter‑Satellite Links
A mesh network with 1 Tbps laser links can route data to the satellite currently over a ground station, decoupling computation from downlink geography. The capacity of this mesh must exceed the product of total compute output rate and the fraction of traffic requiring ground delivery.

