10. Layer 5 — The Reliability Constraint

Orbital hardware cannot be physically serviced within economic bounds. The operational model demands:

· Component‑level reliability: Use of high‑reliability grade components with mean time to failure (MTTF) exceeding mission life by margin.
· Node‑level redundancy: N+1 or N+M redundancy with autonomous failover; each compute blade must be independent.
· Software‑defined resilience: In‑orbit firmware and model updates over the communication links; AI‑driven health monitoring.

Replacement cycle economics: a five‑year lifetime requires the entire system to be re‑launched twice over a decade, doubling the effective launch cost per FLOP‑year. Extending lifetime through radiation hardening and modular servicing (e.g., robotic blade replacement) is a high‑leverage economic lever.

---

11. Layer 5.5 — The Autonomy Constraint

A constellation of thousands of orbital compute nodes cannot be managed by human operators on the ground at fine granularity. The infrastructure itself must be an AI — a self‑monitoring, self‑diagnosing, self‑repairing system. Required autonomous capabilities include:

· Fault detection and isolation: Real‑time anomaly detection across all subsystems, with root‑cause analysis.
· Reconfiguration: Autonomous rerouting of power, thermal loops, and network paths in response to failures.
· Energy management: Optimising solar array pointing, battery cycling, and load shedding based on orbital position and degradation.
· Security: Intrusion detection and response, particularly against cyberattacks that could propagate through the constellation.

Achieving this level of autonomy requires substantial on‑board compute resources dedicated to system management — ironically, an orbital AI must first solve its own operational AI problem.

---

12. Layer 6 — The Economic Constraint

12.1 Total Cost of Ownership Model
Total cost over a 10‑year horizon for an orbital compute system can be expressed as:

\text{TCO} = C_{\text{launch}} + C_{\text{hardware}} + C_{\text{operations}} + C_{\text{replacement}},

where:

C_{\text{launch}} = L \cdot M_{\text{sys}},\quad L \in [100, 3000]\ \text{\$ kg}^{-1}.

Using the mass decomposition (7) and the scaling coefficients from current hardware, a 1 MW system with SSO and advanced radiators yields M_{\text{sys}} \sim 80\ \text{tonnes}. At L=200\ \text{\$ kg}^{-1}, launch cost is $16 million — competitive with terrestrial build costs, but not yet with operational costs when replacement is included.

12.2 Revenue and Price Premiums
The jurisdictional neutrality premium and zero‑water, zero‑carbon operations can support a price per FLOP‑hour 2–5× higher than terrestrial equivalents for specific regulated workloads. This premium, if sustained, creates a viable market entry path even before cost parity is reached.

---

13. Layer 7 — The Systemic Risk Constraint

A large‑scale orbital AI infrastructure becomes a critical global asset, introducing systemic risks that must be priced and mitigated:

13.1 Kessler Syndrome
A fragmentation event in a densely populated orbital shell can trigger a collisional cascade. The probability of such an event scales with the square of the number of objects. For a one‑million‑satellite constellation, even a 0.001 % annual failure rate implies 10 break‑ups per year, requiring active debris removal and design for demise (D4D) as mandatory specifications.

13.2 Space Warfare and Cyber Threats
Anti‑satellite weapons, laser dazzling, and cyber intrusion are realistic threats. A compromised AI node could be used as a launchpad for attacks on the terrestrial internet or other space assets. Resilience requires distributed, heterogeneous architectures and international agreements on space‑infrastructure protection.

13.3 Solar and Cosmic Events
A Carrington‑class event could induce geomagnetically induced currents and single‑event effects across an entire constellation simultaneously. Redundancy alone is insufficient; the system must be designed to weather a correlated failure event through safe‑mode operation and rapid post‑event recovery.

---

14. Layer 8 — The Space Manufacturing Constraint

If the long‑term vision is terawatts of orbital compute, launching every kilogram from Earth is physically and economically untenable. In‑situ manufacturing from lunar or asteroidal materials becomes necessary.

14.1 Fraction of Mass Launched
Define \phi = M_{\text{launched}} / M_{\text{total}}. Currently \phi = 1. The economic crossover occurs when:

L \cdot M_{\text{launched}} > C_{\text{ISM}} \cdot (1-\phi) M_{\text{total}},

where C_{\text{ISM}} is the cost of in‑situ manufactured mass. Early estimates for lunar‑derived aluminium structures suggest C_{\text{ISM}} could be 5–10× lower than Earth launch within two decades, provided a lunar industrial base exists.

14.2 Implications for Radiators and Structures
The largest mass components — radiators, structural trusses, solar array substrates — are precisely those most amenable to simple manufacturing processes (extrusion, rolling, sintering). A scenario where only the high‑value compute chips and specialised optics are launched, while the bulk mass is built in orbit, fundamentally alters the scaling economics.

