Appendix B — Detailed Derivations of Governing Equations

B.1 Stefan–Boltzmann and Effective Sink Temperature

The net radiative heat transfer from a surface at temperature T to a black‑body environment at temperature T_{\text{env}} is

P_{\text{rad}} = \varepsilon\,\sigma\,A\,(T^4 - T_{\text{env}}^4).
\tag{B.1}

In deep space, the cosmic microwave background provides T_{\text{CMB}} = 2.725\ \text{K}. However, a radiator in low Earth orbit receives infrared radiation from the Earth and reflected solar radiation. The effective sink temperature T_{\text{sink}} is defined such that the incident environmental heat flux equals \sigma T_{\text{sink}}^4. For a surface with view factor F to the Earth, the incident Earth infrared flux is approximately F \cdot \sigma T_{\text{Earth}}^4 (with T_{\text{Earth}} \approx 255\ \text{K} effective black‑body temperature). Albedo loading adds a solar contribution. The combined effective sink temperature is then

T_{\text{sink}} = \left( \frac{q_{\text{IR}} + q_{\text{albedo}} + q_{\text{solar}}}{\sigma} \right)^{1/4},
\tag{B.2}

which for typical LEO orientations yields T_{\text{sink}} \approx 180\text{–}220\ \text{K}. This value is used throughout the paper unless otherwise noted.

B.2 Solar Power Generation and Degradation

The instantaneous electrical power from a solar array is

P_{\text{solar}} = \eta\,G_{\text{sc}}\,A_{\text{panel}}\,\cos\theta.
\tag{B.3}

Over time, radiation damage reduces efficiency. Empirically, the efficiency decays as \eta(t) = \eta_0 (1 - d)^t, where d is the annual fractional loss (0.02–0.03 for GaAs in LEO). Hence the end‑of‑life power is

P_{\text{solar,EOL}} = \eta_0 (1-d)^{t_{\text{mission}}}\,G_{\text{sc}}\,A_{\text{panel}}\,\overline{\cos\theta},
\tag{B.4}

with \overline{\cos\theta} the orbit‑averaged cosine factor. For a sun‑synchronous dawn‑dusk orbit with optimal pointing, \overline{\cos\theta}\approx 0.85; for general LEO, it may be 0.6–0.7 after eclipses and pointing losses.

B.3 Link Budget for Optical Communication

The free‑space optical link budget between a satellite and ground station is given by

P_{\text{rx}} = P_{\text{tx}}\,G_{\text{tx}}\,G_{\text{rx}}\,L_{\text{fs}}\,\eta_{\text{atm}},
\tag{B.5}

where the free‑space path loss is

L_{\text{fs}} = \left(\frac{\lambda}{4\pi d_{\text{slant}}}\right)^2.
\tag{B.6}

The antenna gain for a diffraction‑limited aperture of diameter D is

G = \left(\frac{\pi D}{\lambda}\right)^2.
\tag{B.7}

Hence

P_{\text{rx}} = P_{\text{tx}} \left(\frac{\pi D_{\text{tx}}}{\lambda}\right)^2 \left(\frac{\pi D_{\text{rx}}}{\lambda}\right)^2 \left(\frac{\lambda}{4\pi d}\right)^2 \eta_{\text{atm}}
= P_{\text{tx}} \frac{\pi^2 D_{\text{tx}}^2 D_{\text{rx}}^2}{16\,\lambda^2 d^2}\,\eta_{\text{atm}}.
\tag{B.8}

For a photon‑counting receiver, the capacity per beam can approach C \approx P_{\text{rx}} / (h\nu) photons per second, giving data rate

R \approx \eta_{\text{det}} \frac{P_{\text{rx}}}{h\nu}\ \text{bps},
\tag{B.9}

with h\nu = 1.28\times10^{-19}\ \text{J} at 1550 nm and detector efficiency \eta_{\text{det}}\approx 0.7. For a 10 W transmit power, 0.3 m telescopes, and 600 km slant range, P_{\text{rx}} \approx 10^{-8}\ \text{W}, leading to R \approx 50\ \text{Gbps} per beam under clear sky. Atmospheric turbulence and cloud outages reduce availability and average throughput; link margins of 3–6 dB are standard.

B.4 Eclipse Fraction Derivation

For a circular orbit of altitude h and zero inclination, the Earth’s shadow is a cylinder of radius R_E. The angular radius of the Earth as seen from the satellite is \rho = \arcsin(R_E/(R_E+h)). The satellite enters eclipse when the Sun is behind the Earth; the orbital angle of eclipse \theta_e satisfies \cos(\theta_e/2) = \cos\rho / \cos\beta, where \beta is the Sun angle out of the orbital plane. For equatorial orbits at equinox (\beta=0), the eclipse angle simplifies to 2\rho. The eclipse fraction is then

f_e = \frac{\theta_e}{2\pi} = \frac{1}{\pi}\arcsin\!\left(\frac{R_E}{R_E+h}\right).
\tag{B.10}

For h=500\ \text{km}, f_e \approx 0.35. Inclined orbits and seasonal variations alter this; for sun‑synchronous orbits (inclination ~97°–99°), the orbital plane precesses with the Sun, maintaining nearly constant daylight except for brief polar eclipses.

B.5 Battery Mass Calculation

The energy storage required to supply the load during eclipse is

E_{\text{bat}} = P_{\text{load}} \, f_e \, T.
\tag{B.11}

Accounting for depth‑of‑discharge limitations (typically 80 %) and conversion efficiency \eta_{\text{conv}}, the installed battery capacity is

E_{\text{installed}} = \frac{P_{\text{load}} \, f_e \, T}{\text{DOD} \cdot \eta_{\text{conv}}}.
\tag{B.12}

Battery mass:

M_{\text{bat}} = \frac{E_{\text{installed}}}{\rho_{\text{bat}}},
\tag{B.13}

where \rho_{\text{bat}} is specific energy in Wh kg⁻¹. For Li‑ion, \rho_{\text{bat}}=200\ \text{Wh kg}^{-1}, \text{DOD}=0.8, \eta_{\text{conv}}=0.95. For a 1 MW load, 500 km orbit (f_e=0.35, T=5670\ \text{s}), E_{\text{bat}} = 1\times10^6 \times 0.35 \times 5670 \approx 1.98\times10^9\ \text{J} = 550\ \text{kWh}. Then E_{\text{installed}} = 550 / (0.8\times0.95) \approx 724\ \text{kWh}, giving M_{\text{bat}} = 724\,000 / 200 \approx 3620\ \text{kg}.

B.6 Radiator Mass Scaling with Temperature

From Eq. (B.1),

A = \frac{P_{\text{waste}}}{\varepsilon\sigma(T^4 - T_{\text{sink}}^4)}.
\tag{B.14}

Radiator mass M_{\text{rad}} = \rho_A A. To see the temperature sensitivity, consider T_{\text{sink}}=200\ \text{K} and ignore its variation:

M_{\text{rad}} \propto \frac{P_{\text{waste}}}{T^4}.

If the junction temperature is raised from 80 °C (353 K) to 120 °C (393 K), the mass ratio is (353/393)^4 \approx 0.64 — a 36 % reduction. For higher‑temperature operation (e.g., 200 °C with wide‑bandgap semiconductors), mass reduces to 25 % of the baseline.

B.7 Compute‑to‑Communication Ratio Derivation

The CCR is defined as

\text{CCR} = \frac{\text{Total compute operations (FLOP)}}{\text{Bits transmitted to/from ground}}.
\tag{B.15}

For a workload, compute operations are proportional to the data processed and model complexity. For example, an Earth observation payload generates D_{\text{raw}} bits per second of sensor data. On‑board inference reduces this to D_{\text{insight}} bits of output. The CCR is then

\text{CCR} = \frac{C_{\text{inference}} \cdot (D_{\text{raw}} / \text{bit})}{D_{\text{insight}}},
\tag{B.16}

where C_{\text{inference}} is FLOP per input bit. For SAR imagery, C_{\text{inference}} \approx 10^4 FLOP/bit, D_{\text{raw}} \sim 1\ \text{Gbps}, D_{\text{insight}} \sim 1\ \text{kbps}, giving \text{CCR} \approx 10^7.

The viability condition: a workload is orbit‑viable if

\text{CCR} > \frac{\text{Compute rate (FLOP/s)}}{\text{Downlink bandwidth (bps)}}.
\tag{B.17}

This follows from the requirement that the compute output data rate does not exceed the downlink capacity. If it does, the system is communication‑bottlenecked.

B.8 Heat‑to‑Compute Ratio and Radiator Mass

The HCR is the waste heat produced per FLOP of useful computation:

\text{HCR} = \frac{P_{\text{waste}}}{\text{FLOP/s}}.
\tag{B.18}

For an accelerator, P_{\text{waste}} \approx P_{\text{comp}} (all input power becomes heat, ignoring insignificant energy storage). Hence HCR is essentially the inverse of computational efficiency \eta_{\text{comp}} = \text{FLOP/J}. An H100 has \eta_{\text{comp}} \approx 3\times10^{11}\ \text{FLOP/J} (estimated), so \text{HCR} \approx 3.3\times10^{-12}\ \text{J/FLOP}. Future low‑power ASICs might achieve 10^{13}\ \text{FLOP/J}, lowering HCR by 30× and correspondingly reducing radiator mass by the same factor.

Radiator mass directly ties to HCR:

M_{\text{rad}} = \rho_A A_{\text{rad}} = \rho_A \frac{P_{\text{waste}}}{\varepsilon\sigma(T^4 - T_{\text{sink}}^4)} = \rho_A \frac{\text{HCR} \times \text{FLOP/s}}{\varepsilon\sigma(T^4 - T_{\text{sink}}^4)}.
\tag{B.19}

Thus M_{\text{rad}} \propto \text{HCR}/(T^4).

B.9 Mass Scaling Laws

We decompose total system mass:

M_{\text{sys}} = M_{\text{comp}} + M_{\text{power}} + M_{\text{thermal}} + M_{\text{comm}} + M_{\text{struct}}.

Each term is modelled as a linear function of a driving quantity:

· Compute mass: M_{\text{comp}} = k_c P_{\text{comp}}, with k_c \approx 5\text{--}10\ \text{kg kW}^{-1} for dense GPU servers.
· Power mass: M_{\text{power}} = M_{\text{array}} + M_{\text{bat}}. The array mass M_{\text{array}} = \alpha_{\text{PV}} A_{\text{array}}, with \alpha_{\text{PV}} \approx 2\text{--}5\ \text{kg m}^{-2} depending on substrate. Using A_{\text{array}} = P_{\text{elec}}/(\eta G_{\text{sc}}\overline{\cos\theta}), we get M_{\text{array}} = k_p P_{\text{elec}} with k_p \approx 30\text{--}40\ \text{kg kW}^{-1}.
· Thermal mass: M_{\text{thermal}} = \rho_A A_{\text{rad}} = \frac{\rho_A P_{\text{waste}}}{\varepsilon\sigma(T^4 - T_{\text{sink}}^4)}. For P_{\text{waste}} = P_{\text{comp}} and T=353\ \text{K}, \rho_A=3\ \text{kg m}^{-2}, k_t \approx 3.7\ \text{kg kW}^{-1} (much lower if droplet radiators are used).
· Communication mass: antennas, optical heads scale with data rate; approximated as M_{\text{comm}} = k_m R, with R the required downlink rate. Using R = P_{\text{comp}} / (\text{CCR}\cdot\text{HCR}) (from CCR definition and energy per FLOP), M_{\text{comm}} becomes small for high CCR.
· Structure: typically 10–20 % of other masses.

Combined, the scaling coefficient for total mass per unit compute power is the sum of these k’s. This linear model holds until structural efficiency limits arise in megastructures.

