Appendix C — Orbital Viability Index (OVI) Normalisation and Computation

C.1 Normalisation Functions

OVI is defined as

\text{OVI} = \frac{\bar{P}_{\text{solar}} \cdot \text{CCR} \cdot \mathcal{R}}{\bar{A}_{\text{rad}} \cdot L \cdot \mathcal{S}},
\tag{C.1}

where the overbar indicates normalisation to a reference architecture. The baseline is:

· 1 MW compute, LEO, solid‑panel radiators at T=353\ \text{K}, H100‑class GPUs, launch cost $2 000 kg⁻¹.
· \bar{P}_{\text{solar}} = ratio of solar power available per kg of power system to baseline. Baseline: 1 kW kg⁻¹ (equivalent to 40 kg kW⁻¹).
· \text{CCR} is dimensionless; baseline CCR = 10⁴ (typical for inference).
· \mathcal{R} = probability of mission success; baseline 0.95.
· \bar{A}_{\text{rad}} = normalised radiator area per FLOP/s; baseline area per FLOP/s derived from A_{\text{rad}} = 1230\ \text{m}^2 for 1 MW, FLOP/s \approx 3\times10^{17} (H100), so \bar{A}_{\text{rad}} = 1 corresponds to 4.1\times10^{-15}\ \text{m}^2/(\text{FLOP/s}).
· L normalised to baseline $2 000 kg⁻¹, so \bar{L} = L/2000.
· \mathcal{S} = systemic risk factor (probability of catastrophic failure over mission). Baseline \mathcal{S}=0.1.

Thus OVI baseline = 1.

C.2 Example Calculations

SSO, droplet radiator, H100: Assume same compute efficiency (same FLOP/s), but droplet radiator reduces radiator areal density to \rho_A = 0.5\ \text{kg m}^{-2} and area unchanged? Actually droplet radiator eliminates solid panel mass, but the effective radiating area is similar; the key is that thermal mass per kW plummets. In our OVI formulation, \bar{A}_{\text{rad}} is normalised to the baseline radiator area per FLOP/s. If the radiator area is the same (droplet radiators can achieve same area with lower mass), then \bar{A}_{\text{rad}} = 1, but mass penalty is captured in launch cost indirectly because launch mass includes thermal mass. OVI explicitly includes launch cost L; lower mass reduces L proportionally. So if droplet radiator cuts total system mass by 30 %, launch cost per kg is same but total mass is lower; however, OVI uses L as the cost per kg to orbit, not total launch cost. That’s a flaw: OVI should capture the total system economics. We adjust: replace L with a normalised total launch cost factor \bar{C}_{\text{launch}} = \frac{M_{\text{sys}}}{M_{\text{sys,baseline}}} \cdot \frac{L}{L_{\text{baseline}}}. This is more representative.

Revised OVI:

\text{OVI} = \frac{\bar{P}_{\text{solar}} \cdot \text{CCR} \cdot \mathcal{R}}{\bar{A}_{\text{rad}} \cdot \bar{C}_{\text{launch}} \cdot \mathcal{S}}.
\tag{C.2}

Now \bar{C}_{\text{launch}} = (system mass × launch cost per kg) / (baseline system mass × baseline launch cost per kg). For droplet rad, system mass might be 0.7 of baseline, and if launch cost is $500 kg⁻¹, then \bar{C}_{\text{launch}} = 0.7 \times (500/2000) = 0.175. Then OVI = (1 × 1 × 1) / (1 × 0.175 × 1) ≈ 5.7. This aligns with the intuition that lower mass and lower launch cost improve viability.

