Appendix D — Orbital AI Readiness Framework (OARF) — Detailed Rubric

D.1 Scoring Criteria for Each of the 12 Layers

Each layer is scored 1–5. Example for Thermal Maturity (TM):

1. Thermal architecture undefined or passive‑only without area budget.
2. Concept defined but no analysis; area and mass uncalculated.
3. Radiator area and mass budgets closed; ground‑tested deployable radiator.
4. Thermal vacuum test completed with representative heat loads.
5. On‑orbit demonstration at full power and temperature.

Similar rubrics are defined for Energy Maturity (EM), Radiation Maturity (RM), Communication Maturity (CM), Reliability Maturity (ReM), Autonomy Maturity (AM), Economic Maturity (EcM), Workload Alignment (WA), Scaling Maturity (SM), Systemic Risk Maturity (SRM), Space Manufacturing Maturity (SMM), and Orbital Mechanics Selection (OMS). Full criteria tables are available in Supplementary Material.

D.2 Geometric vs. Arithmetic Mean Justification

The aggregate OARF score uses the geometric mean:

\text{OARF} = \left( \prod_{i=1}^{12} S_i \right)^{1/12}.

The geometric mean penalises low scores more severely than the arithmetic mean, reflecting that a single failed constraint (score 1) can render the entire architecture unviable. For example, if 11 layers score 5 and one layer scores 1, the arithmetic mean is 4.33 (deployment‑ready), while the geometric mean is (5^{11}\cdot 1)^{1/12} \approx 3.5 (development). This correctly signals that the architecture is not yet ready. The geometric mean ensures that full mission readiness requires balanced maturity across all constraints.

