Appendix E — Radiation Environment and Shielding Model

E.1 Trapped Radiation Models

The trapped proton and electron fluxes in the Van Allen belts are described by NASA’s AP‑8 (protons) and AE‑8 (electrons) models. For LEO at 500 km, 51.6° inclination, the average integral proton flux >10 MeV is ~10³ cm⁻² s⁻¹ over the South Atlantic Anomaly. The dose rate in silicon behind a shielding thickness x (g cm⁻²) can be approximated by

\dot{D}(x) = \dot{D}_0 e^{-x/x_0},
\tag{E.1}

where \dot{D}_0 \approx 100\ \text{Gy yr}^{-1} unshielded, and x_0 \approx 20\ \text{g cm}^{-2} for protons. A typical shielding of 5 mm Al (1.35 g cm⁻²) gives negligible reduction; 20 mm Al plus water equivalent (~5.4 g cm⁻²) still leaves high dose. A dedicated radiation vault with high‑Z and hydrogenous materials can achieve 30–50 g cm⁻².

E.2 Shielding Effectiveness for GPUs

Total ionising dose (TID) tolerance of commercial GPUs has been tested up to 60 Gy without functional failure (NASA). For a 5‑year mission, the allowable dose rate is 12 Gy yr⁻¹. Using Eq. (E.1), the required shielding mass can be estimated by balancing dose attenuation against mass penalty. The optimal shield often uses polyethylene (high hydrogen content) plus tungsten or aluminium layers.

E.3 SEU Rate Estimation

The SEU rate per bit for a given technology node in a given orbit can be modelled using the Bendel model or empirical cross‑sections. For an HBM2 memory device in LEO, the upset rate is typically 10^{-12} upsets bit⁻¹ day⁻¹. With 80 GB of memory per GPU, that yields ~0.1 upsets GPU⁻¹ day⁻¹. A constellation of 10 000 GPUs would experience ~1000 upsets per day, demanding ECC and soft‑error tolerant software.

