Appendix F — Thermal Resistance Network Analysis

F.1 Heat Path

The total temperature rise from the GPU junction to the radiator surface is the sum of:

· Junction‑to‑case (\Delta T_{jc}): ~10 K for a lidless chip with liquid cooling.
· Case‑to‑cold plate (\Delta T_{cp}): ~2 K with thermal interface material.
· Cold plate to loop evaporator (\Delta T_{\text{evap}}): ~5 K.
· Two‑phase loop pressure drop saturation temperature loss (\Delta T_{\text{drop}}): ~3 K.
· Loop condenser to radiator panel (\Delta T_{\text{cond}}): ~5 K.

Total \Delta T_{\text{total}} \approx 25\ \text{K}. If the radiator surface is at T_{\text{rad}}, the junction temperature is T_j = T_{\text{rad}} + \Delta T_{\text{total}}. To keep T_j \leq 85\ \text{°C} (358 K) with margin, T_{\text{rad}} must be ≤ 333 K. This constrains radiator sizing, as lower T_{\text{rad}} requires larger area.

F.2 Two‑Phase Cooling Loop Design

A mechanically pumped loop using ammonia as working fluid can transport >1 kW per cm² of cold plate area. The mass of the pump, accumulator, and plumbing scales with heat load. For a 1 MW system, the loop mass is estimated at 200–400 kg, added to the radiator mass.

