Appendix G — Scaling Laws and Regime Transitions

G.1 Derivation of Regime Thresholds

Regime boundaries are defined where a key subsystem’s mass or complexity becomes the dominant driver.

· I → II: When passive radiators (body‑mounted) can no longer reject heat and deployable radiators are required. This occurs around 100 kW for typical satellite geometries.
· II → III: When the radiator mass alone exceeds the payload fairing capacity of a single heavy‑lift launch vehicle, necessitating on‑orbit assembly. With solid radiators (\rho_A=3\ \text{kg m}^{-2}), 100 tonnes is reached at about 30 MW waste heat. Droplet radiators extend this limit.

G.2 Launch Cadence Model

For a constellation requiring N satellites of mass m each, with a launch vehicle capacity C and annual cadence f, the deployment time is T_{\text{deploy}} = \lceil N m / C \rceil / f. For one million satellites of 1 tonne each, using Starship (100 t per launch), that’s 10 000 launches. At 500 launches per year globally, 20 years of uninterrupted launch activity. This drives the need for extremely low‑mass satellite designs.

---

Appendix H — Economic Model Details

H.1 TCO Breakdown with Learning Curves

We model launch cost L(t) = L_0 e^{-\alpha t} with a learning rate of 15 % per doubling of cumulative launches. For Starship, starting at $500 kg⁻¹, after 1 000 launches (~10 years), L \approx 160\ \text{\$ kg}^{-1}. Combined with a reduction in satellite mass via technology improvement (\beta = 5\%\ \text{yr}^{-1}), total system TCO decreases super‑exponentially for the first decade. The crossover to terrestrial parity occurs when:

\frac{\text{TCO}_{\text{orbit}}}{\text{FLOP}} \leq \frac{\text{TCO}_{\text{terrestrial}}}{\text{FLOP}}.

Detailed spreadsheets are provided in the supplementary data.

