Appendix I — Planetary Compute Migration — Formal Principle and Historical Verification

Principle of Compute Migration: Computational infrastructure migrates toward physical environments that minimize the product of energy cost, thermal rejection cost, and scarcity rent of required resources, subject to the constraint of acceptable latency for the dominant workload class.

Verification against historical phases:

· Mainframe to PC: latency reduction outweighed energy cost.
· PC to data centre: sharing of cooling and power infrastructure reduced per‑FLOP cost.
· Data centre to cloud: geographic arbitrage of energy and tax.
· Cloud to hyperscale: AI demand pushed energy supply to limit; new sites sought near hydro and nuclear.
· Hyperscale to orbit: Earth’s surface energy density and cooling water limit reached; space offers infinite solar and radiative cooling.

This principle predicts that once orbital compute establishes economic parity for a significant workload class, a self‑reinforcing migration will occur, driving further cost reductions through scale and in‑situ manufacturing. Lunar compute would follow, exploiting the lunar cold traps for passive cooling at 40 K.

