Appendix J — Kessler Syndrome Probability Model

The probability of a collisional cascade in an orbital shell with N objects of cross‑sectional area \sigma and relative velocity v over time \Delta t is

P_{\text{cascade}} \approx 1 - \exp\left(-\frac{1}{2} N^2 \sigma v \frac{\Delta t}{V}\right),
\tag{J.1}

where V is the shell volume. For a shell at 550 km, thickness 50 km, volume \approx 4\pi R_E^2 \times 50\,\text{km} \approx 2.5\times10^{11}\ \text{km}^3. With N=10^6, \sigma=10\ \text{m}^2, v=10\ \text{km s}^{-1}, and \Delta t=1 year, the exponent is \sim 10^{-4}, so annual probability ~0.01 %. However, this scales quadratically; for N=10^7, it becomes 1 % per year — a significant risk mandating stringent debris mitigation.

